DESCRIPTION >
    How per-turn cost and context behave as a conversation gets deeper. Reads billable
    (assistant) turns from agent_message_facts, groups them by turn_index (the stored,
    0-indexed chronological position of a turn within its conversation), and at each depth
    reports the median plus a p25–p75 band and a p95 envelope of (a) per-turn cost and
    (b) per-turn context size (input + cache read + cache write). This is a population view:
    every turn at a given depth across EVERY conversation contributes, so no single
    conversation is singled out and there is no chosen "deep vs shallow" cutoff.

    The headline is a log–log elasticity: a robust Theil–Sen slope of ln(median cost) on
    ln(depth) across the depths that carry enough turns to be meaningful. It answers the
    "slope tells me two things" question with one unitless number:
      ~0  -> flat: per-turn cost does not grow with depth (a few large reads, not bloat)
      ~1  -> linear: each doubling of depth doubles per-turn cost (history re-paid every turn)
      >1  -> accelerating: cost grows faster than depth (the runaway / loop signal)
    cost_elasticity is exp-readable as "each doubling of depth multiplies per-turn cost by
    2^cost_elasticity". context_elasticity is the same fit on context size (the upstream cause).

    Nothing here is a magic cutpoint: depth values are raw stored turn_index; the band/envelope
    are exact quantiles; the elasticity is the median pairwise slope, not chosen. The only knob is
    min_depth_samples — turns at a depth observed in fewer than this many conversations are too
    thin to quantile or fit, so they are excluded from the FIT (not the series) and the series
    carries sample_count so the UI can fade the thin right tail honestly.

NODE turns
SQL >
    %
    WITH
        {{ Int64(start_time_ms, 0) }} AS start_ms_raw,
        {{ Int64(end_time_ms, 0) }} AS end_ms_raw,
        if(end_ms_raw = 0, toUnixTimestamp64Milli(toDateTime64(now(), 3)), end_ms_raw) AS end_ms_candidate,
        if(start_ms_raw = 0, end_ms_candidate - 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000, start_ms_raw) AS start_ms_candidate,
        least(start_ms_candidate, end_ms_candidate) AS start_ms,
        greatest(start_ms_candidate, end_ms_candidate) AS end_ms,
        fromUnixTimestamp64Milli(start_ms) AS start_dt,
        fromUnixTimestamp64Milli(end_ms) AS end_dt
    -- Main-thread turns only. A Claude sub-agent transcript is a separate file that the sync
    -- layer links to the PARENT session_pk (session_pk = hash(source, vendor_session_id), which
    -- ignores agent_id), but its turn_index restarts at 0. So a sub-agent turn and a main-thread
    -- turn can share (session_pk, turn_index): grouping by turn_index would then double-count and
    -- overstate sample_count, and a nested side-quest's depth is not the conversation's depth.
    -- agent_depth = 0 AND is_sidechain = 0 keeps one monotonic turn_index per session_pk, so
    -- (session_pk, turn_index) is unique and sample_count = count() = conversations at that depth.
    SELECT
        turn_index,
        cost_usd,
        toFloat64(input_tokens + cache_read_tokens + cache_creation_tokens) AS context_tokens,
        session_pk
    FROM agent_message_facts
    WHERE OrgId = {{ String(org_id, '') }}
        AND role = 'assistant'
        AND agent_depth = 0
        AND is_sidechain = 0
        AND EventAt >= start_dt
        AND EventAt < end_dt
        {% if defined(sources) %}
        AND source IN splitByChar(',', {{ String(sources) }})
        {% end %}
        {% if defined(models) %}
        AND model IN splitByChar(',', {{ String(models) }})
        {% end %}
        {% if defined(repos) %}
        AND repo_fingerprint IN splitByChar(',', {{ String(repos) }})
        {% end %}

NODE by_depth
SQL >
    -- One row per conversation depth. Cost quantiles use only priced turns (cost_usd is
    -- non-null), so an unpriced turn neither inflates nor zeroes the cost band; context
    -- quantiles use every assistant turn. sample_count is the number of conversations that
    -- reached this depth (= turns at this depth, one per session), so the UI can fade the tail.
    SELECT
        turn_index AS depth,
        count() AS sample_count,
        countIf(cost_usd IS NOT NULL) AS priced_sample_count,
        round(quantileExactIf(0.25)(assumeNotNull(cost_usd), cost_usd IS NOT NULL), 6) AS cost_p25,
        round(quantileExactIf(0.5)(assumeNotNull(cost_usd), cost_usd IS NOT NULL), 6) AS cost_p50,
        round(quantileExactIf(0.75)(assumeNotNull(cost_usd), cost_usd IS NOT NULL), 6) AS cost_p75,
        round(quantileExactIf(0.95)(assumeNotNull(cost_usd), cost_usd IS NOT NULL), 6) AS cost_p95,
        round(quantileExact(0.25)(context_tokens), 0) AS context_p25,
        round(quantileExact(0.5)(context_tokens), 0) AS context_p50,
        round(quantileExact(0.75)(context_tokens), 0) AS context_p75,
        round(quantileExact(0.95)(context_tokens), 0) AS context_p95
    FROM turns
    GROUP BY turn_index

NODE fit
SQL >
    %
    -- Theil–Sen slope of ln(median) on ln(depth+1) over the depths that carry enough
    -- conversations to be a stable quantile. depth+1 keeps depth 0 in the fit (ln(1)=0).
    -- Cost fit uses depths with a positive median priced cost; context fit uses positive median
    -- context. The slope IS the elasticity — derived, not chosen.
    --
    -- We use Theil–Sen (the median of all pairwise slopes), NOT ordinary least squares (ADR 0021
    -- §Relationship). OLS has a 0% breakdown point — one runaway-deep, high-cost depth drags the
    -- fitted slope arbitrarily; on heavy-tailed cost that is exactly the failure mode. Theil–Sen
    -- has a ~29.3% breakdown point, so a single outlier depth cannot move the elasticity. The
    -- point set is one row per charted depth (already sparse-filtered), so the O(d^2) pairwise
    -- enumeration is trivial. Pairs sharing an x (none here, since x = ln(depth+1) is distinct per
    -- depth) are skipped as undefined. With fewer than 2 fit points there is no slope to estimate,
    -- so the elasticity is 0 (flat: the honest default when there is no trend).
    SELECT
        if(length(cost_slopes) >= 1, arrayReduce('medianExact', cost_slopes), 0.) AS cost_elasticity,
        if(length(ctx_slopes) >= 1, arrayReduce('medianExact', ctx_slopes), 0.) AS context_elasticity,
        length(cost_x) AS cost_fit_points,
        length(ctx_x) AS context_fit_points
    FROM (
        SELECT
            cost_x,
            ctx_x,
            -- Pairwise slopes (y_j - y_i)/(x_j - x_i) for every i<j with distinct x; their median
            -- is the Theil–Sen estimate. Empty when < 2 fit points -> elasticity falls back to 0.
            -- The median is taken with `medianExact` (exact order statistic), NOT the `median`
            -- alias, which is the interpolating/sampling quantile — the house estimator is exact
            -- everywhere (ADR 0021 §Distribution), and an even-length slope array must not drift.
            arrayFilter(
                s -> isFinite(s),
                arrayFlatten(
                    arrayMap(
                        i -> arrayMap(
                            j -> (cost_y[j] - cost_y[i]) / (cost_x[j] - cost_x[i]),
                            arrayFilter(j -> j > i AND cost_x[j] != cost_x[i], range(1, length(cost_x) + 1))
                        ),
                        range(1, length(cost_x) + 1)
                    )
                )
            ) AS cost_slopes,
            arrayFilter(
                s -> isFinite(s),
                arrayFlatten(
                    arrayMap(
                        i -> arrayMap(
                            j -> (ctx_y[j] - ctx_y[i]) / (ctx_x[j] - ctx_x[i]),
                            arrayFilter(j -> j > i AND ctx_x[j] != ctx_x[i], range(1, length(ctx_x) + 1))
                        ),
                        range(1, length(ctx_x) + 1)
                    )
                )
            ) AS ctx_slopes
        FROM (
            SELECT
                groupArrayIf(log(depth + 1), sample_count >= {{ Int64(min_depth_samples, 5) }} AND cost_p50 > 0) AS cost_x,
                groupArrayIf(log(cost_p50), sample_count >= {{ Int64(min_depth_samples, 5) }} AND cost_p50 > 0) AS cost_y,
                groupArrayIf(log(depth + 1), sample_count >= {{ Int64(min_depth_samples, 5) }} AND context_p50 > 0) AS ctx_x,
                groupArrayIf(log(context_p50), sample_count >= {{ Int64(min_depth_samples, 5) }} AND context_p50 > 0) AS ctx_y
            FROM by_depth
        )
    )

NODE tail
SQL >
    %
    -- Window-level honesty about the thin deep tail. A depth observed in fewer than
    -- min_depth_samples conversations is too sparse to quantile or fit, so the endpoint marks it
    -- not-well_sampled and the client drops it from the chart (keeping the curve where there is a
    -- real population). These scalars let the footnote state exactly what was set aside, and they
    -- are self-scaling: a dataset of only shallow conversations clips early, a genuinely deep one
    -- extends. observed_max_depth is the true deepest turn (reported even though it is not charted).
    SELECT
        maxIf(depth, sample_count >= {{ Int64(min_depth_samples, 5) }}) AS charted_max_depth,
        max(depth) AS observed_max_depth,
        countIf(sample_count < {{ Int64(min_depth_samples, 5) }}) AS pooled_depth_count,
        sumIf(sample_count, sample_count < {{ Int64(min_depth_samples, 5) }}) AS pooled_turn_count
    FROM by_depth

NODE endpoint
SQL >
    %
    SELECT
        d.depth AS depth,
        d.sample_count AS sample_count,
        d.priced_sample_count AS priced_sample_count,
        {{ Int64(min_depth_samples, 5) }} AS min_depth_samples,
        d.sample_count >= {{ Int64(min_depth_samples, 5) }} AS well_sampled,
        d.cost_p25 AS cost_p25,
        d.cost_p50 AS cost_p50,
        d.cost_p75 AS cost_p75,
        d.cost_p95 AS cost_p95,
        d.context_p25 AS context_p25,
        d.context_p50 AS context_p50,
        d.context_p75 AS context_p75,
        d.context_p95 AS context_p95,
        round(f.cost_elasticity, 4) AS cost_elasticity,
        round(f.context_elasticity, 4) AS context_elasticity,
        f.cost_fit_points AS cost_fit_points,
        f.context_fit_points AS context_fit_points,
        t.charted_max_depth AS charted_max_depth,
        t.observed_max_depth AS observed_max_depth,
        t.pooled_depth_count AS pooled_depth_count,
        t.pooled_turn_count AS pooled_turn_count
    FROM by_depth AS d
    CROSS JOIN fit AS f
    CROSS JOIN tail AS t
    ORDER BY d.depth

TYPE ENDPOINT
